Cyril Slobin wrote:
> 2011/1/30 Jorge Llamb=EDas <jjllambias@gmail.com>:

>>My Loglan dictionary has "gacpi" for Lojban "gleki", and "claso" for
>>Lojban "claxu" (and "nirve" for "kunti"). Are "hapci" and "clesi"
>>typos or are they modern variants?

gacpi became hapci and claso became clesi as part of GMR in 1982.  There

http://www.lojban.org/publications/wordlists/oldlog.txt

has my effort to find the closest TLI equivalent to Lojban gismu, but=20
there are place structure differences, as Cyril has noticed.

Cyril and others are welcome to try to improve on the list, which is of=20
course over 17 years old, and thus even predates the current LLG baseline.

> I haven't studied the Loglan history in deep, and don't know much of
> it's elder versions. All Logli, who are meeting regularly in SL
> (typically three or four -- not a very large community), are using LOD
> (in Java or HTML form), which has "hapci" and "clesi" (and not "gacpi"
> and "claso"). Both versions are downloadable from loglan.org site. As
> far as I know, there is no primitive word for {kunti}, and the
> compound word "nirveo" ("zero contain") lacks the {se kunti} place
> (empty of which material?). I have done my best replacing it with
> "clesi".=20

Of course even in TLI Loglan, you were supposed to be able to coin new=20
lujvo, so you could come up with new ones that have the places that you=20
need, based on what is there.  Limiting oneself to the closed wordlist=20
of LOD makes poetry especially difficult.

